  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 128 PM CDT Thu Aug 20 2026\n\nTODAY \nWeak convergence amid the NW flow aloft will allow for clear \nskies and highs to increase into the 80s-low 90s this afternoon. \nWithin this flow aloft is a shortwave disturbance along the \nMN/Canadian border, supporting a weak surface low and a weak cold \nfront stretched across the Dakotas. This front is expected to \nmove southward, potentially forcing some isolated thunderstorms \ninto central Nebraska after 4PM, suggested by earlier model \nguidance. However, it is uncertain how far the cold front will \nmake it given the marginal upper-level support. In fact, recent \nHRRR and RRFS guidance hast this front stalling out in Northern \nNebraska, keeping thunderstorm chances limited to North of I80. \nShould a thunderstorm form, CAPE values around 2000-2500 J/kg \nand shear in excess of 40 kts could allow some strong to severe \nstorms, with main hazards being golf ball sized hail and strong \nwind gusts of 60+ MPH. While a few thunderstorms are possible,\nmost of the area will remain dry this evening. \n\nTOMORROW (Friday) \nWhile not overly confident, there is a chance of \nfog tomorrow morning, mainly for the eastern half of the area, with \nample low-level moisture and weak surface winds. However, model\nguidance is not as aggressive with this fog as they were this\nmorning. The main uncertainty is the coverage of these evening \nstorms and if skies are able to clear for sufficient radiative\ncooling. Additionally, showers/thunderstorms are possible \n(15-25%) for the NE portions of the area tomorrow morning \nbetween 3-8AM due to some weak WAA advection at 850 mb. A few of\nthese may be on the strong to severe side. Tomorrow afternoon \nis expected to be clear and dry, allowing highs to peak in the \nupper 80s to mid 90s. \n\nAdditional thunderstorms are possible tomorrow during the evening to \novernight hours. Recent model guidance suggests isolated \nthunderstorm development to the North-Northwest of the area around 6-\n8PM, congealing into a complex somewhere over the area. Some of \nthese storms may be strong to severe, as models are indicating 2500+ \nJ/kg of SBCAPE and 45+ kt of 0-6km shear. However, the location and \ntiming of this thunderstorm development remains uncertain. \n\nSATURDAY AND BEYOND\nAs the upper level ridge continues to expand\nacross the intermountain west, mostly dry conditions are \nexpected Saturday, with highs in the 80s. However, the chance \nfor showers/thunderstorms returns Saturday night into Sunday \nmorning as a weak shortwave emerges from the NW flow aloft. \nAdditional off and on showers/thunderstorms are possible into \nearly next week as a broader trough approaches from the West.",
